WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.056 --> 00:00:01.421
(lighthearted music)

00:00:01.421 --> 00:00:03.606
- Yeah, the sheep are everything.

00:00:03.606 --> 00:00:05.961
They're my employees,
they're my co-workers,

00:00:05.961 --> 00:00:07.949
they're my livelihood.

00:00:07.949 --> 00:00:10.366
I'm very much bonded to my animals

00:00:10.366 --> 00:00:11.352
and I hope they're bonded to me,

00:00:11.352 --> 00:00:12.439
hope they enjoy working with me

00:00:12.439 --> 00:00:15.522
as much as I enjoy working with them.

00:00:19.985 --> 00:00:21.570
My name is Zach Richardson

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:23.344
and I'm an urban shepherd.

00:00:23.344 --> 00:00:25.110
I call myself an urban shepherd

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.331
because, I guess,
technically I'm a shepherd,

00:00:27.331 --> 00:00:29.564
one who tends a flock of sheep.

00:00:29.564 --> 00:00:31.879
But most of my projects are in the city.

00:00:31.879 --> 00:00:33.174
(metal clanking)

00:00:33.174 --> 00:00:35.476
(door squeaking)

00:00:35.476 --> 00:00:36.681
Stay.

00:00:36.681 --> 00:00:40.721
Urban grazing is the
strategic use of sheep

00:00:40.721 --> 00:00:44.888
to manage overgrown landscapes
in an urban context.

00:00:46.457 --> 00:00:51.353
Right now, I have a total
of 60 ewes, or female sheep.

00:00:51.353 --> 00:00:54.436
Sheep will eat just about everything.

00:00:55.423 --> 00:00:57.532
This site has about every invasive species

00:00:57.532 --> 00:00:59.407
that's common in middle Tennessee.

00:00:59.407 --> 00:01:03.667
You know, euonymus, English
ivy, privet, honeysuckle,

00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:06.100
Japanese honeysuckle, Tree-of-heaven,

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.694
but they'll eat all of them.

00:01:08.694 --> 00:01:10.978
Let's say you have an
overgrown piece of property.

00:01:10.978 --> 00:01:13.486
You call me and you tell
me exactly the areas

00:01:13.486 --> 00:01:15.127
that you're targeting.

00:01:15.127 --> 00:01:17.888
And then we load up
some sheep on a trailer,

00:01:17.888 --> 00:01:20.014
bring you a modest flock,

00:01:20.014 --> 00:01:24.014
and they stay onsite until
the job is completed.

00:01:25.709 --> 00:01:26.542
(whistling)

00:01:26.542 --> 00:01:31.320
Duggie is the most important
component to my business.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.525
He's more important than my truck,

00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:34.239
he's more important than my trailer,

00:01:34.239 --> 00:01:36.075
he’s almost more important than the sheep.

00:01:36.075 --> 00:01:37.918
Now his job as a Border Collie

00:01:37.918 --> 00:01:40.388
instinctively is to gather the sheep,

00:01:40.388 --> 00:01:41.643
and he loves doing it.

00:01:41.643 --> 00:01:43.272
You could throw a Jr. Whopper
at him when he's working

00:01:43.272 --> 00:01:44.105
and he wouldn't care,

00:01:44.105 --> 00:01:45.655
he just wants to keep working.

00:01:45.655 --> 00:01:47.424
That'll do, here Duggie, lie down.

00:01:47.424 --> 00:01:48.865
'Cause this is not a new concept you know,

00:01:48.865 --> 00:01:50.557
moving sheep to clear land.

00:01:50.557 --> 00:01:53.396
Here in the city we've forgotten
that this is even possible,

00:01:53.396 --> 00:01:54.885
that this is an option.

00:01:54.885 --> 00:01:56.730
It's deep down in all of us somewhere

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:59.556
to want to watch animals do what they do.

00:01:59.556 --> 00:02:02.062
We've just kind of been removed from it.

00:02:02.062 --> 00:02:03.139
It's therapeutic, isn't it?

00:02:03.139 --> 00:02:05.139
Just watching sheep eat.

